WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.280
Film Courage: What were some of the biggest
mistakes you made early on in your filmmaking

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:04.280
career?

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.370
Zeke Zelker, Writer/Director/Producer of the
transmedia project BILLBOARD: Rushing.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:10.889
Early on I would rush through scripts, rush
into production, rush through post so I could

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:13.030
share it.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:17.730
That is probably my biggest problem for instance
I had this problem A.K.A.: It's a Wiley World!

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.289
And then I rush into production because my
Dad always wanted to be in a movie and he

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:22.939
was dying of cancer.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:26.670
I literally would wear my heart on my sleeve
with a lot of things that I do and the reasons

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:33.560
why I do certain things but that was probably
my biggest lesson learned and so now I take

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:34.560
my time.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.790
I believe in story R&amp;D [research and development]
where I like to make things or break things,

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.850
test things in front of audiences and see
what’s working or not working.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.510
For instance with BILLBOARD we have a web
series part of this where we tested it in

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.120
front of a live audience as a play.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.270
I never staged a play before in my life but
I just did it.

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.060
It was successful, it’s crazy.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.600
It was an interactive play where the audience
interacted with the characters via social

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.600
media.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:03.120
On one screen above the billboards was information
that the billboard sitters were putting out,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.090
on another screen was what the audiences were
putting in some of the action on the stage

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.240
was improv based on that correspondence.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.190
I mean the actors weren’t literally Tweeting
and stuff.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.460
We had a whole other team doing that kind
of stuff but it was just very interesting

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.710
and very dynamic and it was an experiment.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:22.210
But it was interesting what we learned from
it was how the audience was reacting to the

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.050
material that would then become the web series.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:29.840
You know you think about it, filmmaking should
really take more of a thought about what TV

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.550
does in terms of “Filmed before a live studio
audience.”

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:34.550
Right?

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:35.550
So just what TV does, what do they do?

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.200
They rehearse it for the first two days, bring
it to a live audience, they tape it four times.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.250
They change some stuff up if something is
not hitting right and they finally do the

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:50.260
taping (the end taping, the last two tapings
I should say).

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.290
And I think filmmaking should take a little
bit of that.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:57.290
Like for instance with BILLBOARD we did sneak
previews this past fall to test our audience

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:01.370
because we thought we knew who our audience
was based off of earlier test screenings and

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:06.241
also doing AB testing with Facebook ads and
so forth but we wanted to make sure that that

00:02:06.241 --> 00:02:10.840
was it and making sure that they responded
to the material and so we did a bunch of sneak

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.410
previews to test how the audience was reacting
to the material and making sure that our test

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:16.870
audience was the actual audience.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:21.950
And in fact I actually ended up shooting two
scenes that end up in the final film.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.400
So I firmly believe in story R&amp;D.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.209
And so now the lesson learned was not rush
but take your time.

00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:32.069
Also too not having all of the resources at
your disposal also warrants taking your time

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:34.030
as well.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:39.810
The other lesson I learned too was a matter
of empowering people to be successful.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:45.650
Teach them all that you can and hand the objective
over to them and make sure they hit their

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.569
tasks.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:51.990
And I still have the same sort of organizational
stuff that I had for my very first film.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.280
You have to realize that I learn by doing.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.400
I didn’t learn this stuff in school because
I had one semester of film school behind me

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.470
so I literally learned by doing.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:06.319
So over the course of the years I think I
became a better and better filmmaker and I’ve

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:07.950
lost my arrogance.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.230
I don’t think filmmakers should be arrogant.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.430
I think they should really listen to their
audience and figure out who they are actually

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:13.549
appealing to.

